-# XGBOOST-TABULAR-FAULTS
+# XGBoost Tabular Faults
+
 > **Category:** Component Fault Classification  
+> **Platform adapter:** `m11-xgboost-tabular`  
+> **Status:** integrated (primary tabular voter)  
+> **Collection position:** model **25** of 25 (alphabetical)
+
+<!-- Generated from deployment/models.catalog.json - edit the catalog, not this file. -->
+
+Gradient-boosted trees that classify multi-class component states from structured sensor features, and the primary subject of the platform's SHAP explanations. It is the strongest single tabular classifier in the collection and the one most alerts end up resting on.
+
+## How this page fits the collection
+
+The WT-PM collection numbers its 25 repositories **alphabetically 01-25** - that is the number on this page - and gives each one a **task-based adapter id** (`m11-xgboost-tabular`) used by the platform registry, the `wt-pm` CLI and the fusion engine. Both are printed on every page so the ecosystem graph and the registry can be read against each other.
+
+- Repository: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-xgboost-tabular-faults>
+- Landing page: <https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-xgboost-tabular-faults/>
+- Ecosystem graph: <https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/ecosystem.html>
+- Platform architecture: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md>
+- Per-model table: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/platform/docs/MODELS.md>
+
+## Model
+
+- `XGBoost classifier in model.py`
+- `sklearn-API wrapper under the platform adapter (xgboost optional)`
+
+| Block | Detail |
+|---|---|
+| Boosting | Trees added sequentially, each fitting the second-order Taylor expansion of the loss - the Newton step that made XGBoost fast |
+| Regularisation | Explicit L1/L2 penalties on leaf weights plus a complexity term per tree |
+| Multi-class head | One-vs-all softmax over component states |
+
+## Advanced concepts
+
+The ideas this model actually leans on - stated with their failure modes, not as marketing.
+
+### The second-order step
+
+Boosting with gradient and curvature information converges in far fewer rounds than first-order boosting on the same data. The price is a tighter coupling between loss and implementation - which is why the fallback to m10 exists.
+
+### Regularisation is what prevents memorising the plant
+
+Leaf-weight penalties and tree complexity terms are the main reason a boosted ensemble does not simply memorise turbine-specific quirks. They are also why the model can be retrained per site without collapsing.
+
+### Explainability by construction
+
+TreeExplainer computes exact Shapley values for this model in polynomial time, so the strongest classifier also produces the most rigorous explanation - the platform's XAI story rests on that pairing.
+
+### Distillation target
+
+Its behaviour is what the m25 edge relay approximates with a depth-5 tree, and what m24 compresses to INT8. Accuracy at the centre buys simplicity at the edge.
 
