+<h1>XGBoost Tabular Faults</h1>
+<p class="lede">Gradient-boosted trees that classify multi-class component states from structured sensor features, and the primary subject of the platform's SHAP explanations. It is the strongest single tabular classifier in the collection and the one most alerts end up resting on.</p>
+<div class="cta">
+<a class="btn" href="https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-xgboost-tabular-faults">View on GitHub</a>
+<a class="btn ghost" href="https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/ecosystem.html">25-model ecosystem graph</a>
+<a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md">Platform architecture</a>
+</div>
+</header>
+<p class="note">This page is generated from <code>deployment/models.catalog.json</code> in the reference repository, together with the other 24 model pages, so all 25 agree on numbering, contract and status.</p>
+<h2>Model</h2>
+<ul>
+<li><code>XGBoost classifier in model.py</code></li>
+<li><code>sklearn-API wrapper under the platform adapter (xgboost optional)</code></li>
+</ul>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Boosting</th><td>Trees added sequentially, each fitting the second-order Taylor expansion of the loss - the Newton step that made XGBoost fast</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Regularisation</th><td>Explicit L1/L2 penalties on leaf weights plus a complexity term per tree</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Multi-class head</th><td>One-vs-all softmax over component states</td></tr>
+</table>
+<h2>Advanced concepts</h2>
+<p class="note">The ideas this model actually leans on, each stated with its failure mode.</p>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>The second-order step</h3>
+<p>Boosting with gradient and curvature information converges in far fewer rounds than first-order boosting on the same data. The price is a tighter coupling between loss and implementation - which is why the fallback to m10 exists.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Regularisation is what prevents memorising the plant</h3>
+<p>Leaf-weight penalties and tree complexity terms are the main reason a boosted ensemble does not simply memorise turbine-specific quirks. They are also why the model can be retrained per site without collapsing.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Explainability by construction</h3>
+<p>TreeExplainer computes exact Shapley values for this model in polynomial time, so the strongest classifier also produces the most rigorous explanation - the platform's XAI story rests on that pairing.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Distillation target</h3>
+<p>Its behaviour is what the m25 edge relay approximates with a depth-5 tree, and what m24 compresses to INT8. Accuracy at the centre buys simplicity at the edge.</p>
+</div>
+<h2>Platform contract</h2>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Input (adapter view)</th><td>features (+ labels in research mode)</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Output</th><td>prediction + probability</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Record format</th><td><code>wt-pm.platform.v1</code></td></tr>
+<tr><th>Typical latency</th><td>800 ms</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Deployment targets</th><td>cloud, edge_cpu</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Requirements</th><td>xgboost, scikit-learn</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Fallback</th><td>m10-random-forest</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Subsystem focus</th><td>whole turbine</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Hermes tool</th><td><code>diagnose / explain</code></td></tr>
+</table>
+<p class="note">Records follow the shared <code>WTDataSchema</code> (<code>wt-pm.platform.v1</code>). Only <code>timestamp</code>, <code>turbine_id</code> and <code>model_id</code> are required; absence is distinguishable from a zero.</p>
+<h2>Platform layers</h2>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Fusion</th><td>Primary tabular vote in the fault-classification ensemble</td></tr>
+<tr><th>XAI</th><td>Primary TreeExplainer subject - the explain tool's default</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Edge</th><td>Distilled into the m25 relay and quantized by m24</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Fallback</th><td>Receives fallbacks from m01 and m12 when their feature views are unavailable</td></tr>
+</table>
+<h2>How it connects to the other 24</h2>
+<ul class="edges">
+<li><b>13. LSTM SCADA Anomaly (reference contract hub)</b> <span class="note">(m05-lstm-scada-anomaly)</span> &rarr; anomaly records for fault triage</li>
+<li>&rarr; <b>22. TinyML ESP32 Safety Relay</b> <span class="note">(m25-tinyml-safety)</span> - distilled depth-5 tree exported to the ESP32 relay</li>
+<li>&rarr; <b>24. XAI SHAP Interpretable</b> <span class="note">(m21-xai-shap)</span> - SHAP explanations of boosted-tree alerts</li>
+</ul>
+<h2>Honest limits</h2><ul class="limits">
+<li>Tabular only - it cannot see raw waveforms or sequence structure</li>
+<li>There are no operator-labelled maintenance records in the collection; the label set comes from the simulator</li>
+</ul>
+<p class="note">Fidelity: rung 1 of the fidelity ladder - simulator data, surrogate waveforms where stated, proxy RUL targets, simulator fault labels. Comparable within this collection, not field-validated turbine control.</p>
